    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Cirques (or Corries) are bowl-shaped depressions formed at the head of glacial valleys.",
        "Aretes are sharp, narrow ridges that divide two adjacent cirques.",
        "Horns are pyramid-shaped mountain peaks, such as the Matterhorn, created by the headward erosion of multiple cirques.",
        "Glaciers carve wide, flat-floored U-Shaped Valleys, often leaving smaller tributary valleys stranded above as Hanging Valleys.",
        "Fjords are drowned glacial valleys submerged by rising sea levels, whereas Roche Moutonnée are asymmetrical rock features."
      ],
      "id": "ksxarae"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Arete – sharp ridge between two cirques.",
      "id": "okgruf9"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Horn – pyramid-shaped peak (e.g., Matterhorn).",
